  "query_preview": "..\n\nZonal westerly flow aloft south of an upper-level trough in\nCentral Canada will continue transporting moisture into the \nregion, producing cloudy skies today. \n\nAn Eastern-Pacific trough will move onshore over Oregon by\ntomorrow morning before digging over Western Montana by mid-\nafternoon. Instability and low-level moisture associated with the\ntrough will likely produce rain across northeast portions of \nMontana Sunday afternoon through Monday morning. Precipitation \ntotals will range between 0.01\" and 0.25\".\n\nSE surface winds will reach speeds of 20 to 25 mph sustained Sunday\nafternoon, with gusts potentially reaching 35 mph, therefore a \nLake Wind Advisory has been issued. Winds will veer westerly \nMonday as zonal flow aloft becomes predominant and the trough \nbegins exiting eastward. Winds of comparable strength reaching \nadvisory criteria are expected. Wave heights have the potential to\nreach 4.5 ft, particularly Monday afternoon.\n\nThe trough will fully exit the region overnight Monday while the \nzonal flow left in its wake begins building into a weak ridging\npattern. Slight chances for rain showers are possible Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning due to a swath of low-level moisture\nmoving throughout. Confidence in rain amounts are low. Rain \nchances dimish Wednesday afternoon as skies clear and temperatures\nwarm under the ridge through the remainder of the week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nThere is moderate to high confidence in sustained winds at or \nabove 20 mph on Sunday and Monday across Fort Peck Lake.\n\nThere is low confidence in rain amounts Tuesday night into\nWednesday.\n\n\nDON",
